```sas
ods pdf file="path\to\journal.pdf" template="journal";
title "Journal Title";
 proc print data=sashelp.cars;
run;
ods pdf close;
```
In this example:

* `ods pdf file="path\to\journal.pdf"` specifies the output file name and location.
* `template="journal"` specifies the journal template. SAS provides several built-in templates, including "journal", "oman", and "report". You can also create your own custom template.
* `title "Journal Title"` sets the title of the journal.
* `proc print data=sashelp.cars;` runs the PRINT procedure on the SASHELP.CARS dataset.
* `run;` executes the PRINT procedure.
* `ods pdf close;` closes the PDF file.

You can customize the journal template by adding more `ods` statements or modifying the template file. For example, you can add headers, footers, and section breaks using `ods` statements like `ods region`, `ods title`, `ods date`, and `ods section`.

Alternatively, you can use the `ods rtf` statement to output a Rich Text Format (RTF) file, which can also be converted to PDF.

You can also use the `ods` statement with the `pdf` option and specify other options such as `startpage` and `stoppage` to control the layout and formatting of the output.

For example:
```sas
ods pdf file="path\to\journal.pdf" template="journal" startpage=none stoppage=none;
title "Journal Title";
proc print data=sashelp.cars;
run;
ods pdf close;
```
